every language is a product of its  history and that's certainly the case  when we look at creole languages new  languages which arise as a result of  contact between groups of people who  share no common language one such creole  is papiamento a portuguese-based creole  spoken in the dutch caribbean papiamento  is spoken by around 340 000 people  mainly on the abc islands aruba bonaire  and kurasaw despite their location in  the caribbean sea these islands are part  of the kingdom of the netherlands both  dutch and papua mento are official  languages in aruba in kurasaw while only  dutch is official in bonaire but  papiamento is the most common language  used for daily interaction that might  sound kind of strange why is a  portuguese-based creole spoken alongside  dutch rather than portuguese the process  of how papiamento developed is not  entirely known but it's likely that it  developed from a portuguese creole  spoken in the portuguese colonies of  west africa and was spoken by enslaved  people taken to the caribbean this seems  likely because popiamento bears a strong  resemblance to upper guinea creoles  spoken in the cape verde islands  guinea-bissau and casamance in senegal  with portuguese exploration in the 15th  and 16th centuries came the  establishment of trading posts and  colonies which were part of the  portuguese empire this included those on  the west african coast and cape verde  islands as a result of contact between  the portuguese and africans both locals  and enslaved africans taken there from  elsewhere pidgin languages arose in  order to expedite communication between  the different groups these pigeon  languages consisted of mainly portuguese  words and improvised stripped-down  grammar when the children of pidgin  speakers began to speak a pidgin  language as their native language they  became stable creole languages  these portuguese colonies in west africa  were huge centers of the transatlantic  slave trade and most of the enslaved  people taken to the abc islands departed  from there a large number of them must  have spoken a portuguese-based pigeon or  creole language and brought it with them  to the caribbean where it underwent  further influence the abc islands were  under spanish control from 1527 to 1634  when many people were forcibly taken  there from africa and there has always  been extensive contact between the  islands and the south american mainland  which is just a stone's throw away so  there has naturally been some spanish  influence on papiamento the dutch took  control of the islands in 1634 and have  ruled them ever since so there has  naturally been some dutch influence on  papiamento and there was likely also  influence from judeo-portuguese because  many of the residents of the islands  were sephardic jews from brazil portugal  and elsewhere populamento is generally  considered a portuguese-based creole but  with significant spanish influence as  well on top of the 20 of papua mental  vocabulary that comes from dutch  approximately 80 percent of papua mental  vocabulary is iberian in other words  spanish or portuguese but because the  two languages are closely related it's  not always clear which language a word  came from  some words are clearly from portuguese  here are a couple of examples  meaning butterfly from  meaning power from forza  some words are clearly from spanish a  couple of examples are sombre meaning  hat from sombrero  meaning trousers from  which means underpants  and as i mentioned a moment ago some  words are of less certain origin  meaning which from either spanish  or portuguese  meaning how much from either spanish  cuanto or portuguese  a couple of commonly used words of dutch  origin are  meaning book  meaning apple from apple  as you may have expected there are also  words derived from west african  languages for example meaning teneal  this comes from the wolf word jongoto  there are some words from taino or  arawak the indigenous language spoken on  the islands before colonization though  some of these first entered spanish then  papiamento for example maishi meaning  corn from the taino or arawak word mahis  but which entered popiamento from the  spanish word maith  and there were also some words from  english  meaning bottle bicycle meaning bicycle  and the short word is base meaning bike  here are a few basic greetings in  papiamento bundia good morning compare  that to portuguese  good afternoon compare that to  portuguese  good evening compare that to portuguese  notice that in papiamento the word for  good is bone in each case even though in  portuguese there's both  and  that's because portuguese has masculine  and feminine gender whereas papiamento  doesn't have grammatical gender  one more greeting  how are you  i'm good  kon means how like  in portuguese or komo in spanish ta is  the present tense marker and bai means  go and comes from portuguese  the third person singular form of  meaning to go so this is like how's it  going me means i ta is the copula and  bon means good  kon is a question word that resembles a  portuguese one or a spanish one so are  kua meaning which and cuanto meaning how  much which we saw before this is true  for other question words as well  when this comes from portuguese  or spanish  this comes from portuguese king and  spanish  what this one is derived from spanish k  meaning what in spanish  meaning thing  what are you doing  i'm reading a book  why this word comes from a combination  of spanish para meaning for and the word  we just saw kiku meaning what  what time compare this to portuguese  chiaris  what day compare this to portuguese  of course the spanish phrases are very  similar too  the basic word order in papiamento is  svo subject verb object as we saw in  this sentence  here's the subject here's the verb and  here's the object note that the subject  is a personal pronoun most of the  personal pronouns resemble portuguese  and spanish ones me i this comes from  portuguese  the prepositional form of the pronoun  this comes from portuguese  a mostly archaic second person plural  subject pronoun and or spanish bush a  second person singular pronoun still  used in dialects with boseo  he or she from portuguese  and or spanish  this particular pronoun is not from  spanish or portuguese it's original to  papiamento it might seem strange that  i'm saying portuguese and or spanish  right i mean how could it be both well  words can sometimes have a dual  etymology in which a word originates in  one language and is reinforced by a  similar word in another language  it's interesting to note that while in  portuguese and spanish the subject  pronoun can be dropped in popiamento it  can't that's because in portuguese and  spanish the verb has conjugations that  indicate person and number but in  papiamento the verb isn't conjugated for  person in number  when it comes to verb tenses the  popumental verbs don't normally change  form but a tense or aspect marker can be  placed before the verb as is quite  common in creole languages  this is the present tense marker which  actually comes from spanish  and or portuguese  this is the future tense marker this is  an abbreviation of the portuguese word  logu which means soon it normally comes  before the subject  i will sing but lo is normally only used  to really emphasize the future the  present tense is often used instead  this is the past tense marker it  actually comes from the auxiliary verb  in the spanish present perfect ah the  third person singular form  this marks habitual action or ability to  do something  i sing with the meaning of on a regular  basis or i know how to sing  sa comes from portuguese and spanish  meaning to know  this means going to and is also used for  the future  i'm going to sing  when you came in i was singing  for the present progressive there's  actually a present participle form  that's used  i'm reading a book lesando is the  present participle for