 and we're  going to eat some fish creole  notice the difference in the final vowel  in juntu next notice that the k sound is  represented by c in aruba but k in  bonaire and kurasaw and notice the word  for and is spelled with a y in aruba and  an i in bonaire  now let's break the other elements of  the sentence down word for word it's  john present tense marker go together  with me to my grandmother her house and  we future tense eat fish creole tabai is  the present tense of go huntu means  together and comes from portuguese  or spanish  means with and comes from portuguese  or spanish  means my grandmother's house willa comes  from spanish abuela which means  grandmother su is used to connect the  possessor and the thing that's possessed  literally it's my grandmother her house  but in natural english it's my  grandmother's house lokome is the future  tense of the verb meaning eat pisca is  the noun meaning fish but it comes from  the portuguese and spanish verbs  meaning to fish  now let's break down a couple more  sentences  this is the book which i bought word for  word it's this present copula the book  which i past marker by  izaki means this and comes from spanish  este this plus aki meaning here ku is a  relative pronoun meaning which or that  it comes from portuguese  and or spanish  means by and comes from portuguese  in spanish  and one final sentence  i like this house but it's too expensive  word for word it's i like the house here  but it present copula too expensive  gusta means like and comes from spanish  gusta  next ikasaki means this house in the  previous sentence we looked at isaki  meant this but when we need to say it in  a phrase like this house then it's  phrased as the house here  pero means but and comes from old  portuguese peru or spanish peru muchu  comes from spanish mucho and means much  or many as well as tu  karu means expensive and comes from  portuguese  and could have been reinforced by  spanish karo  as you can see papiamento is a lot like  other creole languages with the majority  of its vocabulary coming from its main  lexifier language portuguese but with a  lot of grammatical simplifications and  with different spellings and word forms  that make it immediately obvious that  this is not portuguese and then there  are all the other influences that  collectively help make papiamento  something unique of its own the question  of the day for speakers of papiamento  what differences do you notice between  the different varieties of your language  as it's spoken on the different islands  and for other people what's your  impression of papiamento leave your  answers in the comments down below and  it's time to say thank you to all the  langfocus patreon supporters especially  the top tier patrons whose names appear  right here on the screen many extra  special thanks go out to them learn how  to become a patron by visiting  patreon.com  langfocus and to everyone thank you for  watching and have a nice day  [Music]  you

